1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,137
Pay off debt, or save first?

2
00:00:02,557 --> 00:00:09,677
It's the question everyone asks. The answer is: both, in a specific order. Here's the order, and the math behind each step.

3
00:00:10,447 --> 00:00:11,286
Five steps.

4
00:00:12,056 --> 00:00:14,362
Step one. A starter emergency fund.

5
00:00:14,782 --> 00:00:25,726
Before you attack debt, save one month of bills. In our example, $2,371. Without it, the next car repair goes right back on the card, and you pay off the same debt twice.

6
00:00:26,496 --> 00:00:28,619
Step two. Grab any employer match.

7
00:00:29,039 --> 00:00:38,187
If your employer matches retirement contributions, that's often the best return you'll ever see. Say they match fifty cents per dollar, up to six percent of pay.

8
00:00:38,607 --> 00:00:50,084
On $60,000, 6% is $3,600 a year from you, and $1,800 from your employer. An instant 50% return. Even a 24% card can't beat that.

9
00:00:50,504 --> 00:00:52,152
Plans vary, so check yours.

10
00:00:52,922 --> 00:00:54,869
Step three. Kill high-interest debt.

11
00:00:55,289 --> 00:01:05,705
Credit cards, store cards, anything around 10% or more. Every dollar here earns a guaranteed return equal to the rate. Episodes 2 and 11 have the plans.

12
00:01:06,475 --> 00:01:08,758
Step four. Finish the emergency fund.

13
00:01:09,178 --> 00:01:17,352
Now build it to 3–6 months of essentials. In our example, 3 months is $9,033. Episode 10 shows how.

14
00:01:18,122 --> 00:01:22,506
Step five. Then decide: lower-interest debt, or saving for bigger goals.

15
00:01:22,926 --> 00:01:34,098
A car loan at 7% versus savings at 4%? Paying the loan earns more. But savings stays flexible. With low-rate debt it's a judgment call, and both are good choices.

16
00:01:34,518 --> 00:01:35,125
Recap.

17
00:01:35,285 --> 00:01:37,123
Starter fund. Employer match.

18
00:01:37,283 --> 00:01:40,241
Kill high-interest debt. Finish the emergency fund.

19
00:01:40,401 --> 00:01:41,595
Then choose your goals.

20
00:01:42,365 --> 00:01:51,382
The Paycheck Planner in the description tracks the emergency fund and the debt in one place. This is general education, not financial advice.

21
00:01:51,802 --> 00:01:54,557
Subscribe for more money math, with real numbers.
